; AX3022: a macro parameter used as a `set` target was given
; something that is not a variable.
;
; `(set name v)` names a binder rather than evaluating an expression:
; a template binder answers its renamed name and a macro parameter
; answers its argument's name, which exists only if the argument is
; itself a plain identifier. A parameter standing in that position
; can be handed any expression at all, so the case is real and is
; refused rather than dropped - with poison, not the parameter's
; name, so the template's own identifier never reaches the tree.
; The well-formed twin (a variable argument) draws nothing: the
; store lands on the caller's binding.
(macro (setit t v) (set t v))

(:: main Int)
(fn (main)
  (let ((mut x 1))
    {
      (setit (+ x 1) 2)
      (setit x 3)
      0
    }))
